.TH GIT-CLOSE-BRANCH 1 "August 2026" "Noble Factor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-close-branch \- switch to the target branch, pull, and delete merged feature branches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-close-branch
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR branch ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR target ]
.RB [ \-m | \-\-merged ]
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.RI [ branch ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-close-branch
automates the cleanup after feature branches have been merged.
It deletes each selected branch locally and on the remote, switching the main
worktree to the target branch only when that worktree holds one of them.
.PP
Branches may be named as trailing operands, selected repeatedly with
.BR \-b ,
or discovered with
.BR \-\-merged .
With no selection at all the command closes the branch you are standing on,
which is its original single-branch behavior.
.PP
The command performs the following steps:
.IP 1. 3
Resolves the set of branches to close, excluding the target branch
.IP 2. 3
Moves to the main worktree, so the caller's own location cannot block the removal of a worktree
.IP 3. 3
Checks out the target branch there and pulls once, but
.B only
when one of the selected branches is checked out in the main worktree. Git refuses to delete a branch
from a worktree holding it, and that is the sole reason for the switch. A branch living in a linked
worktree needs none: the worktree is removed, then the branch is deleted. An unrelated main worktree is
left as found, uncommitted changes and all.
.IP 4. 3
Surveys each branch \(em what exists, what it would cost, and whether the close may proceed \(em and
decides. Nothing is removed until every check has passed, so a refusal leaves every trace of the branch
where it was.
.IP 5. 3
Removes what the survey found, in one order:
.BR origin ,
then the worktree, then the local branch. The remote goes first because it is the trace that fails for
reasons having nothing to do with the branch \(em no network, an expired credential, a protected ref \(em
and failing there leaves all three intact. The worktree follows, because discarding it is the one
irreversible act. The local branch is last, because
.B git branch \-d
refuses a branch a worktree holds.
.IP 6. 3
Reports what was closed and what failed, naming which trace failed and what had already been removed
.PP
When the main worktree does hold a selected branch and cannot be switched \(em uncommitted changes \(em
only the branches that needed the switch fail. The rest are closed, and the message names the worktree and
the branch it is holding.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-branch " " \fIbranch\fR
A feature branch to close. May be repeated. Equivalent to naming the branch as a trailing operand.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target " " \fIbranch\fR
The parent branch to switch to. Defaults to the repository's default branch (e.g., main or master).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-merged
Add every local branch whose pull request GitHub reports as
.BR MERGED .
Requires the
.BR gh (1)
CLI and issues one query per local branch.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print, for each selected branch, what the run would do: the delete flag it would receive and any worktree it
would remove, or the refusal that would stop it. The preview is the same survey the run acts on, printed
rather than executed, so it cannot disagree with the run. A refused branch names no removal, because a
refusal removes nothing. Changes nothing.
.SH FAILURE HANDLING
Each branch is closed independently. A branch that cannot be closed is reported and the run continues, so one
unmerged branch in a set does not strand the rest. The exit status is non-zero if any branch failed.
.SH FORCE DELETION
A squash merge creates one new commit on the target with a different SHA, so the branch's own commits never
enter the target's history and
.B git branch \-d
refuses a branch that was in fact merged. Reachability cannot answer the question, and neither can
.BR "git cherry" ,
whose patch\-id comparison only matches when the branch held a single commit.
.PP
This command therefore escalates to
.B \-D
only when
.B two independent signals agree:
.IP 1. 3
.BR gh (1)
reports the pull request
.BR MERGED ,
.B and
the target's history carries a commit whose subject references that pull request \- the "(#<number>)" suffix
GitHub appends when it squashes. A stale or misresolved
.B gh
answer cannot force a delete by itself.
.IP 2. 3
Nothing exists only locally: the tip matches
.IR origin/<branch> ,
or the remote branch is already gone. If the remote exists and differs, the local branch holds commits origin
never received, and forcing would discard the only copy.
.PP
A branch failing either signal is refused and reported, with the reason distinguishing the two cases, and the
run continues to the next branch.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Close a branch the safety checks refuse. The flag is read once, while the branch is surveyed, so every
refusal it overrides is resolved before anything is removed. With this flag the branch is deleted with
.BR "git branch \-D" ,
unconditionally: it overrides the command's own refusals \(en commits that exist only here, or a pull
request GitHub reports merged that the target branch carries no commit for \(en and it also overrides
.BR "git branch \-d" 's
refusal of a branch not merged into HEAD, which is every branch whose work reached the target by
cherry-pick or squash rather than by merge. The run says what it discards before it does. Without the flag
those branches are skipped and reported.
.IP
The flag also passes through to
.BR git\-worktree (1),
which refuses a worktree holding changed or untracked files. Without
.BR \-\-force
that worktree is left alone and its branch is reported as failed, naming the number of files in the way;
with it the worktree is removed and the same number is reported as discarded.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success \- every selected branch closed.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error \- missing or invalid options, or no branches to close.
.TP
.B 65
Data error \- one or more branches could not be closed.
.TP
.B 69
Unavailable \-
.B \-\-merged
was requested without the
.BR gh (1)
CLI.
.TP
.B 75
Temporary failure \- the target branch could not be pulled.
.SH EXAMPLES
Close the current branch after merging:
.PP
.nf
git-close-branch
.fi
.PP
Close several branches at once:
.PP
.nf
git-close-branch fix/nullglob-shell-rc docs/plan-phase-3 xdg-package
.fi
.PP
See what a cleanup would do before doing it:
.PP
.nf
git-close-branch --merged --dry-run
.fi
.PP
Close every merged branch:
.PP
.nf
git-close-branch --merged
.fi
.PP
Close a branch and switch to a specific target branch:
.PP
.nf
git-close-branch -b hotfix/urgent-fix -t staging
.fi
.SH WORKFLOW
Typical usage after a feature PR is squash-merged:
.IP 1. 3
Create feature branch off the default branch
.IP 2. 3
Push feature branch, create PR
.IP 3. 3
Squash-merge PR on GitHub
.IP 4. 3
Run: git-close-branch \-\-merged
